### What does this question ask?

The interviewer wants you to combine two streams and handle duplicates. This tests `Stream.concat`, deduplication patterns, and awareness of when to use concat vs flatMap.

### How do you merge two lists with Stream.concat?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.stream.*;

public class ConcatDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> mumbai = List.of(
            new Employee("E001", "Rajesh",  "TRADING"),
            new Employee("E002", "Priya",   "RISK"),
            new Employee("E003", "Amit",    "OPERATIONS"));

        List<Employee> delhi = List.of(
            new Employee("E002", "Priya",   "RISK"),
            new Employee("E004", "Kavita",  "COMPLIANCE"),
            new Employee("E005", "Suresh",  "OPERATIONS"));

        // Concat: all Mumbai first, then all Delhi
        List<Employee> merged = Stream.concat(
                mumbai.stream(), delhi.stream())
            .toList();

        System.out.println("Merged size: " + merged.size());
        merged.forEach(e -> System.out.println(e.getId() + ": " + e.getName()));
    }
}
```

```text
Merged size: 6
E001: Rajesh
E002: Priya
E003: Amit
E002: Priya
E004: Kavita
E005: Suresh
```

E002 (Priya) appears twice because concat does not deduplicate.

### How do you deduplicate after concat?

```java
// Method 1: toMap with merge function
Map<String, Employee> dedupMap = Stream.concat(
        mumbai.stream(), delhi.stream())
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(
        Employee::getId,
        e -> e,
        (first, second) -> first));  // keep Mumbai (first) on conflict

List<Employee> deduped = new ArrayList<>(dedupMap.values());

// Method 2: distinctByKey filter
List<Employee> deduped2 = Stream.concat(
        mumbai.stream(), delhi.stream())
    .filter(distinctByKey(Employee::getId))
    .toList();
```

### How is concat different from flatMap for combining streams?

| Feature | Stream.concat(a, b) | flatMap(identity) |
|---|---|---|
| Number of streams | Exactly 2 | Any number |
| Syntax | Stream.concat(s1, s2) | Stream.of(s1, s2, s3).flatMap(s -> s) |
| Nesting | Deeply nested for 3+ streams | Flat always |
| Performance | Lopsided tree degrades for many levels | Flat and efficient |
| Best for | Combining exactly 2 streams | Combining 3+ streams |

```java
// Two streams: concat is clear
Stream<Employee> two = Stream.concat(mumbai.stream(), delhi.stream());

// Three or more: flatMap is better
List<Employee> pune = List.of(new Employee("E006", "Neha", "IT"));
Stream<Employee> three = Stream.of(
        mumbai.stream(), delhi.stream(), pune.stream())
    .flatMap(Function.identity());

// WRONG: deeply nested concat for many streams
Stream<Employee> nested = Stream.concat(
    Stream.concat(mumbai.stream(), delhi.stream()),
    pune.stream());  // works but builds lopsided tree
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake 1: reusing streams after concat.

```java
// WRONG: streams can only be consumed once
Stream<Employee> mumbaiStream = mumbai.stream();
Stream<Employee> merged1 = Stream.concat(mumbaiStream, delhi.stream());
merged1.forEach(System.out::println);

// This throws IllegalStateException: stream already operated on
Stream<Employee> merged2 = Stream.concat(mumbaiStream, delhi.stream());
```

Mistake 2: expecting concat to deduplicate automatically.

```java
// WRONG assumption: concat does NOT remove duplicates
List<Employee> wrong = Stream.concat(mumbai.stream(), delhi.stream())
    .toList();  // still has duplicate E002!

// CORRECT: explicitly deduplicate
List<Employee> correct = Stream.concat(mumbai.stream(), delhi.stream())
    .filter(distinctByKey(Employee::getId))
    .toList();
```

### What about preserving order during dedup?

toMap with HashMap does not preserve insertion order. Use LinkedHashMap to maintain the concat order.

```java
LinkedHashMap<String, Employee> ordered = Stream.concat(
        mumbai.stream(), delhi.stream())
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(
        Employee::getId,
        e -> e,
        (first, second) -> first,
        LinkedHashMap::new));

List<Employee> orderedList = new ArrayList<>(ordered.values());
// [E001, E002, E003, E004, E005] in concat order
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- concat creates a lazily concatenated spliterator. No elements are copied until the terminal operation consumes them.
- If either input stream is parallel, the resulting stream is parallel.
- For very large merges, consider database-level UNION instead of in-memory concat.
- The distinctByKey pattern with ConcurrentHashMap is parallel-safe; the toMap approach with a merge function is also parallel-safe.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you merge and sort?" Chain sorted() after concat: `Stream.concat(a, b).sorted(comparing(salary))`.
- "How do you merge with priority (take newer record)?" Use a merge function that checks a timestamp field.
- "What is the time complexity?" O(n + m) for concat plus O(n + m) for dedup, so O(n + m) overall.
